# RHEL Kernel Rollback Runbook

Procedure for testing and rolling back to an older kernel on a RHEL 9/10 system (GRUB2 + BLS + grubby), used on the Satellite server.

## Background

- `grubby --info=ALL` lists installed kernels and their index/id.
- `grub2-reboot` sets a **one-time** override (`next_entry` in `/boot/grub2/grubenv`) — used for the next boot only, then auto-clears back to the normal default. Safe way to test a kernel.
- `grubby --set-default` writes the **persistent** default (`saved_entry` in `grubenv`) — stays until changed again.
- Switching kernels only changes the running kernel (`uname -r`). It does **not** change `/etc/os-release` or any installed RPMs — those stay at whatever version they were.

## 1. Prerequisites

- Hypervisor snapshot (if virtualized) or confirmed out-of-band console access (iDRAC/iLO/IPMI) if physical.
- Maintenance window, since this is a production Satellite server.

## 2. Identify available kernels

```bash
sudo grubby --info=ALL
```

Note the `index`, `kernel` path, and `title` for the kernel you want to test.

## 3. Back up current boot config

```bash
sudo cp /boot/grub2/grubenv /root/grubenv.bak
sudo cp -r /boot/loader/entries /root/loader-entries.bak
```

## 4. Record current state

```bash
uname -r
sudo grubby --default-kernel
sudo grub2-editenv list
```

## 5. One-time test boot (safe, auto-reverts)

```bash
sudo grub2-reboot "<exact title from grubby --info=ALL>"
sudo grub2-editenv list        # confirm next_entry is set
sudo reboot
```

After reboot:

```bash
uname -r                              # confirm the test kernel is running
sudo satellite-maintain health check  # confirm services are healthy
```

Since only `next_entry` was set (not `saved_entry`), rebooting again with no further action returns you to the original default automatically.

## 6. Make the change permanent (only after successful test)

```bash
sudo grubby --set-default /boot/vmlinuz-<version>
sudo grubby --default-kernel     # confirm it took
sudo reboot
uname -r                         # confirm
```

## 7. Revert back to the original kernel

```bash
sudo grubby --set-default /boot/vmlinuz-<original-version>
sudo grub2-editenv list          # confirm saved_entry points back to original
sudo reboot
uname -r                         # confirm reverted
```

## Quick command reference

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| List all kernels/indexes/ids | `sudo grubby --info=ALL` |
| Show current default | `sudo grubby --default-kernel` |
| Show saved/next boot entries | `sudo grub2-editenv list` |
| One-time test boot (by index) | `sudo grub2-reboot <index>` |
| One-time test boot (by title) | `sudo grub2-reboot "<title>"` |
| Set persistent default | `sudo grubby --set-default /boot/vmlinuz-<version>` |

## Notes specific to this run (satellite server)

- Kernels available: 9.8 (`5.14.0-687.26.1.el9_8.x86_64`, index 0), 9.3 (`5.14.0-362.8.1.el9_3.x86_64`, index 1), rescue (index 2).
- `grubby` has no one-time-boot flag — only `grub2-reboot` supports that, via the separate `next_entry` grubenv variable.
- `/etc/os-release` stayed at 9.8 throughout, since kernel switching doesn't touch installed packages — expected behavior, not an error.
